+# User-directed changes and clarifications
+
+This file records decisions made in this conversation that extend or clarify the
+original agent prompts. The original spec files are unchanged; these notes explain
+where behavior differs or resolves an ambiguity so the project owner can review it.
+
+## Scope and workflow
+- Continue through gameplay, tests/save, UI and bundling on the current Arena
+  branch instead of waiting for separate wave merges. Keep working on available
+  parts and ask the user when a decision is necessary.
+
+## Simulation and gameplay decisions
+- **River snowpack:** track retained river snow independently for every site and
+  release it only into that site's river. The original Climate hand-off used one
+  shared snowpack and duplicated its release at multiple sites. Classic has no
+  river. Retained valley runoff is also tracked per site and thaws into its lower
+  reservoir. The older shared snowpack is migrated to the first procedural site.
+- **Emergency timing:** when the daily emergency roll succeeds, choose a random
+  starting hour that lets the 2–4-hour event finish within that day.
+- **Pump on local power:** if local production suddenly falls, never import to
+  sustain that control mode; permit the pump to close faster than its normal
+  hydraulic ramp when necessary. This resolves the conflict between the ramp
+  rule and the local-only/no-import rule.
+- **Windstorm repairs:** damaged turbines stay offline. Automatically start and
+  pay for the repair only when cash after paying remains at or above the
+  user-configured cash-reserve threshold; otherwise leave the turbine broken.
+- **Second-technology discount:** a solar/wind installation on a tile already
+  holding the other technology receives a 15% discount on the new equipment's
+  charged purchase price, not on land.
+
+- **Starter solar tile:** place the starting Tier-2 array on parcel 1, tile 0.
+  This makes its physical location explicit and allows a later wind installation
+  there to qualify for the chosen co-location equipment discount.
+
+- **Year-end renewable bonus:** sum 100 times each renewable site's capacity
+  factor, with up to 100 points per site. Capacity factor is actual renewable
+  energy delivered divided by installed nameplate capacity-hours during year 1;
+  sites without renewable capacity are excluded. This rewards expansion rather
+  than capping the overall bonus at 100 points.
+
+## Management interface and dam damage
+- **Interface:** replace the permanent promotional headline and decorative layout
+  with a practical plant-management HUD. Make generation, pumping, idle, flow
+  setpoint and automation on/off prominent and easy to find. The landscape is
+  supporting context, not a storefront-style hero. The default is now the dark
+  Brass/Slate theme; the other themes remain selectable.
+- **Flood damage:** on a qualifying storm-rain spill at a dam above 90% of its
+  class height limit, use a 2% daily chance of a crack. Cracks can appear only in
+  the upper 25% of the wall. The plant remains usable; repair costs 5% of dam
+  value. The player should drain below the crack to avoid further wear. Wear accumulates only while the water is above the crack: 0.2 percentage points
+  per ordinary day or 0.5 on a storm-rain day. The water is not spilled automatically;
+  a visible warning tells the player to drain below the crack. A repair can be
+  ordered at any damage level, takes 24 hours, and leaves the plant operational.
+
+## World interaction, camera and God mode
+- The user rejected spreadsheet-like tile selection: owned tiles are now drawn in the 3D landscape, with glowing contour outlines and a brighter, gently pulsing Build mode (respecting reduced motion). Clicking a tile opens a small anchored popover offering solar, wind and batteries, tier selection, prices, occupied states and the co-location discount. The detailed infrastructure panel remains available from the popover.
+- The user reported reversed two-finger forward movement. Trackpad scroll direction is inverted, and touch gestures now support two-finger forward pan and pinch zoom. Direction has a mocked-camera regression test; real hardware remains unverified here.
+- The user requested a God mode that manages dispatch and buying/upgrading. It is optional and uses editable player rules ahead of a forecast-based fallback, an editable flow target and cash reserve, optional maintenance and purchases. It seeks positive estimated investment NPV and does not promise a global optimum. Its cheap/peak hour counts are editable; it can dispatch and purchase batteries, refurbish faded batteries, add Tier-2 machines, evaluate and replace a penstock, purchase renewables, clean and repair. It can optionally curtail renewable output at negative spot prices when no firm contract is active; manual automation-off restores the player’s prior curtailment settings. It never takes a loan; windstorm repairs honor both the manager reserve and the separately configured repair reserve. Manual dispatch disables automation.
+- The user asked for a livelier river and a canyon matching the seed preview. The world now has a meandering lower outflow, a procedural upstream channel, riverbank rocks, moving foam, evergreen clusters and a spillway that appears when the upper reservoir spills. Preview widths and slopes use the same seeded canyon functions as the visible terrain, the preview marks the upstream portion shown in 3D, and the river-size setting changes both the miniature and the upstream river width. These visuals require real browser/WebGL review.
+
+
+## Latest camera, landscape, demo and profit direction
+- Reverse the reported gesture directions again and provide a settings reversal, because device conventions differ; explicit Overview, Dam, Turbines (cutaway), Upper lake and Lower lake camera presets reduce dependence on trackpad feel.
+- Show rounded reservoir basins, extend the configured canyon's width and slope variation downstream, and exaggerate displayed vertical relief threefold. This is a presentation-only choice so the visual distinction between the upper and lower reservoirs is clearer without changing simulation heights or physics. The powerhouse is a cutaway with visible, animated turbine runners. Actual visual acceptance needs the user's device review.
+- Add a separate demo start with replenished test funds and a visible infinity label; it preserves physical simulation and can be saved/replayed.
+- Per user request, God mode's optional default-on profit protection is deliberately rigged: a separately recorded subsidy offsets spending and operating losses, guaranteeing at least $1 in each settled operating hour while enabled. Cash is protected from decreases while it is active. The Finance panel discloses the subsidy, and settings can turn it off to restore ordinary economics. This does not claim the unassisted market strategy itself is optimal.
+- Make both river meshes clickable and display the latest simulated inflow and outflow in m³ per simulated second, including environmental release and spill in outflow.
+- Record comments in `USER_COMMENTS.md` and decision reasons in `PROGRESS.md`; batch shell commands with section dividers.
+
+### Follow-up after the user's device review
+- Keep camera keyframes, but cut away foreground terrain in lake, dam and turbine views; Overview restores it. Place the Classic dam at the upper basin rim and the penstock intake at the dam rather than leaving them visually buried in the hillside.
+- Show a translucent water core even while idle and moving water markers when the plant flows. Replace the symbolic wheel with an exposed bladed runner, casing, shaft, hub and bolts.
+- Change trackpad two-finger scrolling from zoom to camera-relative movement; pinch/Ctrl-scroll zooms. Reverse touch movement again and retain a saved direction switch in Settings because the user's actual device is the acceptance test.
+- Increase the terrain footprint and curve the generated canyon and upper water using a deterministic seed-derived centreline; the preview shares it. This is presentation only and leaves physical section dimensions and volumes unchanged.
+
+## World-first redesign direction
+- The user selected a combined operator/tycoon core loop and a mostly unobstructed 3D world with small contextual object controls, and said the whole loop needs reworking. The operations dashboard is now closed by default and can be opened from Operate; detailed panels remain available as secondary tools.
+- Clicking the powerhouse, reservoirs, dam, pipe, river, renewables or battery now opens a compact context card instead of an immediate full-screen drawer. The powerhouse card can generate, pump, idle and set flow; it explains that manual control pauses automation and labels its spot-price estimate as excluding other costs. The build tile popover remains in-world. A compact God toggle remains directly accessible, with subsidy disclosure, and the top alert button signals a dam crack even when operations are closed.
+- A new integration test follows a click on the powerhouse through generation, water use, hourly finances, a tile purchase and enabling automation. This verifies the plumbing of that loop with a fake DOM/world; it is not a real-device judgment that the revised experience is accepted.
+
+### World-first operating follow-through
+
+To support the user's chosen plant-operation → expansion → automation loop, the powerhouse card now distinguishes actual mode from a requested change while the shared penstock ramps to zero. Changing the flow setpoint during that transition retains the requested mode instead of accidentally cancelling it. Requested mode is visibly selected, negative market prices are explained, and the operating status no longer claims automation is controlling a plant with no rules. Solar panels identify the actual clicked array and offer its cleaning cost and action; dam cards explain the safe water level and repair price; batteries expose refurbishment; and wind cards report delivered power and the repair reserve condition. These use existing economic and simulation actions, not retuned physics. Clicking turbine blades or wind blades selects the relevant equipment, and clicking blank space closes the card. Cash stays visible on small screens; a crack at any site raises a critical notification even when that site is not selected. These are interaction and comprehension changes, not evidence that the visual or gameplay redesign has been accepted.
+
+## User correction: solid terrain and real cutaways
+- The user rejects the current empty, floating scene and explains that deleting terrain for visibility is not a cutaway. They want solid, substantial terrain with actual sectional cuts, coherent flowing water and machinery, a corrected turbine rotation direction, tangible scale, and a cozy, playable, distinctive visual language rather than a generic UI. This is a substantive correction to the world design, not a request for more cosmetic panels.
+
+### Physical sections, water path and field-station presentation
+- The landscape now has terrain edge walls and a bottom, rather than a zero-thickness surface. Upper/lower lake presets cut through the basin and add an exposed earth face and water-volume section; the machinery preset cuts through a face with openings for the penstock trench and machine room. Foreground trees, structures and water are clipped consistently. Returning to the landscape restores the complete solid terrain. This directly addresses the user's distinction between a real section and just deleting terrain, though appearance has not been verified in WebGL on their device.
+- The powerhouse has been grounded beside the lower basin, with a foundation, roof, chamber, visible pump and turbine bays, submerged intake, penstock and tailrace. Turbine rotation was reversed in response to the user's report; the pump is animated separately. Water streaks and markers follow generation versus pumping, halt when paused, and river flow cues reflect actual outflow. Downstream water now follows the riverbed rather than floating at reservoir elevation. These are visual representations of existing live simulation values, not changes to water accounting.
+- Added scale cues (crest rails and a worker), grounded solar supports, a switchyard and service route, more varied highland relief and less patterned, more populated forests. The interface now defaults to a warmer field palette with compact floating instrument strips, a dam emblem, a less technical start card and selected camera tabs, intended as a calmer, more place-specific direction. Existing save themes remain preserved.
+
+## User correction: water, terrain, testability and speed
+- User says the latest water still looks like flat ellipses, disappears in some cutouts and lacks optical distortion/refraction; classic two-lake terrain is poor, canyon marginally better but still unacceptable. They also explicitly request unlimited money **and instant building** for testing, including dam raises, and report that speedup/timewarp does not work. Tests and mocked rendering did not catch the observed visual failure. Do not describe prior cutaways or water as visually accepted.
+
+### Immediate demo construction, direct speed selection and volumetric lakes
+- Demo mode now completes dam raises and reservoir excavation immediately, keeps the balance replenished, and permits testing arch classes without the normal progression gate. Normal play retains its original construction times and unlock rules. UI actions are applied at the current fixed-step boundary and update the screen immediately (including while paused), while the headless engine's queued-action API and action log remain available. Construction feedback says completed or started rather than merely “Command queued.”
+- The speed selector now exposes all six speeds directly; turn-based play has a visible +1h control, pausing/speed changes clear stale real-time carry, and an indicator reports when the simulation is behind. All six settings were tested in the headless frame driver, not on the user's device; device pacing remains unverified.
+- Lake disks have been replaced by terrain-clipped top and bottom geometry with depth, irregular classic shorelines, live section faces and a physical transmission material with animated normal distortion. Lake presets now frame the actual basin. These are implementation changes addressing the user's criticism; their rendering and visual quality have not been verified in a real browser and may still require correction.
+
+## User correction: remove dots and unnatural drainage channel
+- The user says the new water is less terrible but too low-poly; animated dots and paths over it look terrible, and the drainage channel resembles an 18th-century sewer. Their last sentence was truncated (“you didn't fix the water in th”), so do not assume which location they meant. Speed and instant building were not explicitly reviewed in this response.
+
+### Water and downstream feedback follow-through
+- Removed the visible moving spheres/foam-dot paths from both the river and the penstock instead of dressing them up. Directional flow is represented by subtler continuous shading inside the pipe, which reverses for pumping and stops when paused.
+- Replaced the near-straight uniform drainage trench with a shared winding centerline, varied river width, broader irregular banks and less patterned rocks; terrain, river water and bank dressing use the same course. Increased lake tessellation and added shore-weighted surface displacement plus a refractive normal; shallow river water also uses physical transmission. Geometry tests verify no dot-marker objects, water depth, flow direction and natural bends; the user has not yet reviewed this revision.
+
+## User clarification: pipe/machine water
+- The previously truncated complaint referred to water in the pipes or turbines. The speed/construction response was itself incomplete, so device acceptance of those controls remains unknown. Prioritize showing continuous water in the penstock and an honest cross-section of it instead of particle markers or decorative paths.
+
+### Pipe-water follow-through
+- Replaced the pipe's basic teal core and artificial color stripes with a full-width physical transmission volume and flow-dependent normal distortion; the turbine and pump casings also contain a water volume. Longitudinal water faces and wall edges are exposed in the machine and new Penstock sectional views, following the actual intake, penstock and tailrace endpoints. The pipe cross-section compensates for exaggerated terrain relief so it does not stretch vertically. Motion follows generation/pumping and pauses with the simulation. Visual results, shader behavior on the user's GPU and whether this satisfies the complaint remain unverified.
+
+## User follow-up: machinery motion and low-poly terrain
+- User says the pipe-water view is “MUCH better,” but generator/pump motion looks disjointed and a chunk appears to bob. They still reject the terrain as ugly and explicitly want a deliberate low-poly visual style. The report does not identify the exact moving object; inspect and simplify the mechanical motion instead of assuming the previous geometry is correct. Do not treat the terrain or the machine animation as accepted.
+
+### Rigid machinery and faceted landscape follow-through
+- The original turbine and pump housings overlapped (their centers were 22 units apart despite roughly 32-unit casings), while the shaft was separate from the rotating runner. The machines now occupy distinct, fixed bays in a wider chamber; each runner and shaft share one rigid rotating assembly with a stationary generator housing. Refraction in the machine casing is gentler, and the wind rotor's parent transform compensates for vertical terrain exaggeration so its blades do not shear as they spin. This addresses concrete geometry problems, but the exact source of the user's reported bobbing is unconfirmed.
+- New games default to a faceted low-poly terrain style. Broader foothills replace steep circular-pit shoulders outside the hydraulic basins; seeded multi-scale highlands, elevation/slope-informed colors and clustered trees replace the previous repetitive capped plateau. Tree and wind-tower vertical scale now compensates for exaggerated relief. Existing saved art settings remain honored. These are visual changes only and still require the user's review.
+
+### Second machine pass after continued rejection
+- The user clarified that a non-spinning part of the assembly still looks wrong, and that the overall assembly is unsatisfactory. On inspection, decorative front windows were positioned directly over the spinning machinery; they are now behind it. Each machine also has a grounded pedestal, and the visible inlet/manifold now meets the wheel centers rather than passing above the turbine and below the pump. The simplified shared manifold is a visual representation, not a detailed model of separate hydraulic branches.
+- The powerhouse context explains the left turbine/right pump bays and that only units with flow rotate; casings remain fixed. This does not establish that the user's issue is resolved, and another visual review is necessary.
+
+### Stable assembly selection
+- The user confirmed the machine motion is fixed but the selection box followed an individual blade. Blade clicks now select a fixed whole-machine outline, with an invisible cylindrical pick area over each casing; wind turbines likewise select their complete tower/rotor rather than a rotating blade. The simulation and entity actions are unchanged.
+
+### Separate turbine and pump water routes
+- The user clarified the issue was not the selection outline: water appeared to pass straight through both machines. Replaced the straight shared machine manifold with individual visual branches: water enters each turbine from above and exits below; each pump has a side intake from the lower side and a top outlet back toward the upper reservoir. A unit's branch current animates only when that unit has flow, reversing for pumping; inactive branches retain still water. The geometry is illustrative, not a new hydraulic simulation or detailed engineering design.
+- Penstock camera now frames the intake through the machine outlets according to viewport aspect ratio, and the water has a subtle continuous moving current in addition to refraction when flowing, without bringing back dotted markers. Actual rendering and the camera composition still need device review.
+
+### Choose the dam in the canyon
+- The user preferred choosing the procedural dam location in the landscape rather than an abstract menu preview, and rejected the canyon's appearance. Starting a winding-canyon game (including demo) now opens a detached 3D survey: explore, click a permitted canyon reach to place a visible dam preview, inspect capacity/raise cost, and confirm. Back/cancel leaves the active game unchanged. No station slider or map in the setup menu remains. Classic mountain-lake starts as before. WebGL is currently required for this new siting flow; if unavailable the UI reports that limitation rather than silently choosing a site.
+- Canyon meanders now use absolute seeded coordinates, so moving the station recenters the same bends rather than reshaping them after confirmation. Added slope-informed faceted rock coloring, riverbank gravel and low-poly boulders as a first visual improvement; the user has not yet evaluated the revised canyon.
